_Last checked: 23 November 2026. Every figure below was read from the publication it links to. These are survey estimates, not counts of businesses._

## At a glance

1. **The ONS says 30% of UK businesses used AI in September 2026.** All business sizes, Business Insights and Conditions Survey (BICS) Wave 165, fieldwork 21 September to 4 October 2026, published 8 October 2026 ([ONS](https://www.ons.gov.uk/businessindustryandtrade/business/businessservices/bulletins/businessinsightsandimpactontheukeconomy/8october2026)).
2. **The government AI adoption survey says 32%.** Private sector businesses with 5 or more employees, 3,503 interviews between 7 January and 21 April 2026, published 29 October 2026 ([AI adoption survey 2026](https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/ai-adoption-survey-2026/ai-adoption-survey-report-2026-executive-summary)).
3. **The UK Business Data Survey says 41%.** Only businesses that handle digitised data (4,090 of 4,450 interviewed), October 2025 to January 2026, published 18 June 2026 ([UK Business Data Survey 2026](https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/uk-business-data-survey-2026/uk-business-data-survey-2026)).
4. **Business size changes the figure a lot.** In the ONS September 2026 data, 28.7% of businesses with 0 to 9 employees used AI, against 50.9% of those with 250 or more ([ONS chart data](https://www.ons.gov.uk/generator?uri=/businessindustryandtrade/business/businessservices/bulletins/businessinsightsandimpactontheukeconomy/8october2026/a52309bf&format=csv)).

All three are official and none is wrong. Each counts a different group of businesses in a different period. For AI search usage rather than business adoption, see my [AI search statistics](/blog/ai-search-statistics/).

## Which figure can I cite?

Use the row that matches the group you are writing about, and quote its base and month with it.

| Figure | Who is counted (base) | When | Source | Published |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **30%** | Businesses of all sizes in the BICS sample that had not permanently stopped trading, Wave 165 (9,933 responses) | September 2026 (fieldwork 21 September to 4 October) | [ONS, Business insights and impact on the UK economy](https://www.ons.gov.uk/businessindustryandtrade/business/businessservices/bulletins/businessinsightsandimpactontheukeconomy/8october2026) | 8 October 2026 |
| **38%** | Businesses with 10 or more employees, same survey | September 2026 | [ONS, same bulletin](https://www.ons.gov.uk/businessindustryandtrade/business/businessservices/bulletins/businessinsightsandimpactontheukeconomy/8october2026) | 8 October 2026 |
| **35%** | Businesses with 10 or more employees, BICS Wave 159 (sample 38,637; 10,311 responses) | June 2026 | [ONS, Artificial intelligence in UK businesses: 2023 to 2026](https://www.ons.gov.uk/businessindustryandtrade/business/businessservices/articles/artificialintelligenceinukbusinesses/2023to2026); sample and responses from the [ONS bulletin of 2 July 2026](https://www.ons.gov.uk/businessindustryandtrade/business/businessservices/bulletins/businessinsightsandimpactontheukeconomy/2july2026) | 20 July 2026 |
| **32%** | Private sector businesses with 5 or more employees, 3,503 interviews; counts piloting | 7 January to 21 April 2026 | [AI adoption survey 2026, Department for Business, Innovation, Science and Trade](https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/ai-adoption-survey-2026/ai-adoption-survey-report-2026-executive-summary) | 29 October 2026 |
| **41%** | Businesses that handle digitised data (4,090 of 4,450 interviewed) | October 2025 to January 2026 | [UK Business Data Survey 2026](https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/uk-business-data-survey-2026/uk-business-data-survey-2026) | 18 June 2026 |

{% chart type="bar" eyebrow="Five official figures" title="Share of businesses using AI, by survey, base and period" data="ONS all businesses Sep 2026:30|ONS 10 or more employees Sep 2026:38|ONS 10 or more employees Jun 2026:35|Government survey 5 or more employees 2026:32|Business Data Survey data handlers 2025 to 2026:41" suffix="%" source="ONS (8 Oct 2026 and 20 Jul 2026), AI adoption survey 2026 (29 Oct 2026), UK Business Data Survey 2026 (18 Jun 2026)" /%}

The ONS March and June 2026 waves sit between the survey months above, so the ONS series also answers "what was it in spring 2026" (see the chart in the next section).

## Why 30%, 32% and 41% differ

Four things change the number. None of them is an error.

1. **Who is counted.** The ONS 30% includes businesses with 0 to 9 employees. The government survey interviewed private sector businesses with at least 5 employees and left out public administration, education and health. The 41% covers only businesses that handle digitised data, which the survey puts at 86% of UK businesses. The ONS gives 38% for businesses with 10 or more employees in the same September 2026 survey that gives 30% for all sizes.
2. **When it was asked.** The three surveys cover September 2026, January to April 2026 and October 2025 to January 2026. Inside the ONS series alone, all-business use was 25.9% at the March 2026 wave and 29.7% at September 2026 ([chart data](https://www.ons.gov.uk/generator?uri=/businessindustryandtrade/business/businessservices/bulletins/businessinsightsandimpactontheukeconomy/8october2026/a52309bf&format=csv)).
3. **How "uses AI" is defined.** The ONS does not define AI as one concept: it counts a business that reports any technology on a list. The government survey counts businesses "currently using it (including piloting)" at least one AI technology. The Business Data Survey defines AI as "technologies such as generative AI models (e.g. ChatGPT, Copilot and Gemini), machine learning, computer vision and other similar systems".
4. **Who answers and how.** The ONS BICS is a voluntary survey of private sector businesses. The government survey was a telephone survey of the person responsible for technology. The Business Data Survey used telephone and online interviews run by Ipsos.

The ONS also warns that estimates "can vary substantially depending on the measurement approach". It notes that a firm-level working paper, Firm Data on AI, reports much higher firm adoption from surveys of senior business executives, and says higher estimates reflect "a broader, top-down view" of whether firms use any AI technology across their operations. In the same [ONS article](https://www.ons.gov.uk/businessindustryandtrade/business/businessservices/articles/artificialintelligenceinukbusinesses/2023to2026), 55% of employees reported using AI for work or education (Opinions and Lifestyle Survey, Great Britain, 6 May to 28 June 2026), against 35% of businesses with 10 or more employees (BICS Wave 159, June 2026). Those compare people with businesses, so they do not conflict.

If you are weighing what this means for AI visibility work at a UK business, my [AI search optimisation service](/services/ai-search-optimisation/) starts from the buyer questions, not from a headline adoption percentage.

## Adoption over time

The ONS has asked the AI question since September 2023. Its Figure 1 chart data gives the series by size band for every wave since then. All-business use was 9.4% in September 2023 and 29.7% in September 2026 ([chart data](https://www.ons.gov.uk/generator?uri=/businessindustryandtrade/business/businessservices/bulletins/businessinsightsandimpactontheukeconomy/8october2026/a52309bf&format=csv)). The ONS describes the June to September 2026 change as "broadly stable" ([bulletin](https://www.ons.gov.uk/businessindustryandtrade/business/businessservices/bulletins/businessinsightsandimpactontheukeconomy/8october2026)).

{% chart type="line" eyebrow="ONS BICS, all waves" title="Share of businesses using at least one AI technology, September 2023 to September 2026" series="All businesses;250 or more employees" data="Sep 23:9.4;18.7|Dec 23:9.9;19.1|Mar 24:13.8;23.8|Jun 24:12.7;25.4|Sep 24:14.7;30.4|Dec 24:16.1;28.2|Mar 25:17.8;31.2|Jun 25:20.5;36|Sep 25:23.5;39.4|Dec 25:24.7;44.4|Mar 26:25.9;44.6|Jun 26:28.9;48.6|Sep 26:29.7;50.9" suffix="%" source="ONS, Figure 1 chart data, Business insights and impact on the UK economy, 8 October 2026. Points are plotted in the middle of each wave." /%}

The government survey runs a separate series. It reports 16% of businesses with 5 or more employees using AI in 2025 and 32% in 2026, and says its second wave was designed to allow comparison with the first (3,503 interviews in 2026, 3,500 in 2025) ([report](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6ae202189bca21f1c5036e99/artificial-intelligence-adoption-research-report-2026.pdf)). Do not join that series to the ONS one: the two start from different bases.

## AI adoption by business size

Larger businesses report more AI use in every source. The size bands do not line up across surveys, so compare rows loosely and never across columns as if they were one series.

| Size group | ONS BICS, all businesses, September 2026 | Government AI adoption survey, 2026 | UK Business Data Survey, 2025 to 2026 |
|---|---|---|---|
| Smallest | 0 to 9 employees: **28.7%** | 5 to 9 employees: **27%** | Micro, up to 9 employees: **41%** (sole traders **40%**) |
| 10 to 49 employees | **36.1%** | **34%** | Small: **51%** |
| 50 to 249 employees | 50 to 99: **45.8%**; 100 to 249: **50.4%** | **47%** | Medium: **58%** |
| 250 or more employees | **50.9%** | **52%** | Large: **82%** |

Bases: ONS, businesses in the BICS sample ([chart data](https://www.ons.gov.uk/generator?uri=/businessindustryandtrade/business/businessservices/bulletins/businessinsightsandimpactontheukeconomy/8october2026/a52309bf&format=csv)); government survey, private sector businesses with 5 or more employees, 3,503 interviews ([report](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6ae202189bca21f1c5036e99/artificial-intelligence-adoption-research-report-2026.pdf)); Business Data Survey, businesses that handle digitised data, base 4,090 ([report](https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/uk-business-data-survey-2026/uk-business-data-survey-2026)).

{% chart type="bar" eyebrow="ONS BICS, September 2026" title="Share of businesses using AI by employee size band" data="0 to 9:28.7|10 to 49:36.1|50 to 99:45.8|100 to 249:50.4|250 or more:50.9" suffix="%" highlight="250 or more" source="ONS, Business insights and impact on the UK economy, Wave 165, published 8 October 2026" /%}

### The 51% for large businesses: verified for 250 or more

The ONS bulletin states 51% in two ways. The main points and the Figure 1 title say 51% for businesses with 250 or more employees. The body text says "around half (51%) of businesses with 100 or more employees".

The ONS chart data publishes bands of 100 to 249 (50.4%) and 250 or more (50.9%), and no combined 100 or more band. The 51% matches the 250 or more band, so I use that wording and leave out the 100 or more version. No 100 or more band is published, so I cannot confirm or rule out that wording. A combined 100 or more figure would sit between 50.4% and 50.9%. The [Wave 165 dataset](https://www.ons.gov.uk/economy/economicoutputandproductivity/output/datasets/businessinsightsandimpactontheukeconomy) agrees: for 250 or more employees, "not sure" is 24.0% and "does not currently use" is 25.1%, which leaves 50.9% using AI.

## AI adoption by sector

Sector figures come from four different bases, so this table lists each source separately.

| Source and base | Information and communication | Other sectors quoted |
|---|---|---|
| [ONS bulletin](https://www.ons.gov.uk/businessindustryandtrade/business/businessservices/bulletins/businessinsightsandimpactontheukeconomy/8october2026), BICS all businesses, September 2026 | **60%**, "the highest proportion of any industry surveyed" | None in the bulletin text |
| [ONS article](https://www.ons.gov.uk/businessindustryandtrade/business/businessservices/articles/artificialintelligenceinukbusinesses/2023to2026), June 2026 (Wave 159), all-size businesses; the article does not state the base beside the figures, but the Wave 159 dataset industry rows give the same 58% and 13% | **58%** | Construction **13%** |
| [Government AI adoption survey](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6ae202189bca21f1c5036e99/artificial-intelligence-adoption-research-report-2026.pdf), private sector businesses with 5 or more employees, 2026 | **61%** | Business services and administration **39%**; construction **25%**; hotel and catering **23%** |
| [UK Business Data Survey](https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/uk-business-data-survey-2026/uk-business-data-survey-2026), businesses that handle digitised data, 2025 to 2026 | **62%** | Professional, scientific and technical **54%**; administrative and support **51%** |

Information and communication is the highest sector figure quoted in each source. The lowest quoted are construction in the ONS article (13%) and hotel and catering in the government survey's chart (23%).

## What businesses use AI for

The three sources ask about use in different ways.

- **Technology type ([ONS](https://www.ons.gov.uk/businessindustryandtrade/business/businessservices/articles/artificialintelligenceinukbusinesses/2023to2026), businesses with 10 or more employees).** In June 2026, large language model text generation was used by 18%, visual content creation by 16%, machine learning data processing by 12%, machine learning image processing by 6% and robotics by 2%. In September 2026 the figures for text generation and visual content were 21% and 19% ([bulletin](https://www.ons.gov.uk/businessindustryandtrade/business/businessservices/bulletins/businessinsightsandimpactontheukeconomy/8october2026)).
- **Business area ([government survey](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6ae202189bca21f1c5036e99/artificial-intelligence-adoption-research-report-2026.pdf)).** Among businesses that use or plan to use AI, administration was named by 81%, marketing by 71% and IT by 66%.
- **Purpose ([Business Data Survey](https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/uk-business-data-survey-2026/uk-business-data-survey-2026), base 4,090 businesses that handle digitised data).** Researching information was reported by 28%, and summarising in-house information or drafting reports or correspondence by 21%. Among businesses that used AI (base 1,870), 21% said the tools were integrated into existing business systems.

{% chart type="bar" eyebrow="Government AI adoption survey 2026" title="Business areas using or planning to use AI, share of businesses that use or plan to use AI" data="Administration:81|Marketing:71|IT:66" suffix="%" highlight="Marketing" source="AI adoption research report 2026, published 29 October 2026; interviews 7 January to 21 April 2026" /%}

The [ONS article](https://www.ons.gov.uk/businessindustryandtrade/business/businessservices/articles/artificialintelligenceinukbusinesses/2023to2026) notes a depth limit in its own data. Among businesses with 10 or more employees using AI, the average number of technologies used rose only from around 1.4 to around 1.6 between September 2023 and June 2026.

## Barriers to AI adoption

- **Skills ([government survey](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6ae202189bca21f1c5036e99/artificial-intelligence-adoption-research-report-2026.pdf)).** 67% of businesses that had adopted AI (1,224 interviews) said limited AI skills, expertise or knowledge had been a barrier.
- **No clear case ([government survey](https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/6ae202189bca21f1c5036e99/artificial-intelligence-adoption-research-report-2026.pdf)).** Among businesses neither using nor planning to use AI (2,043 interviews), 65% cited no clear business case or proven return on investment. Separately, 38% of all businesses did not consider AI relevant to their organisation.
- **No barriers ([ONS](https://www.ons.gov.uk/businessindustryandtrade/business/businessservices/articles/artificialintelligenceinukbusinesses/2023to2026), 10 or more employees, June 2026).** 41% reported no barriers to adoption in the last three months. Among businesses with 100 to 249 employees, around 18% said lack of expertise had delayed adoption.

The ONS "41% no barriers" is a different measure from the Business Data Survey 41% above. They share a number and nothing else.

## Methodology and sources

| Source | Publisher | Published | Fieldwork and sample | Method |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| [Business insights and impact on the UK economy, 8 October 2026](https://www.ons.gov.uk/businessindustryandtrade/business/businessservices/bulletins/businessinsightsandimpactontheukeconomy/8october2026) | Office for National Statistics | 8 October 2026 | Wave 165, 21 September to 4 October 2026; sample 38,592, 9,933 responses | Voluntary survey; official statistics in development |
| [Artificial intelligence in UK businesses: 2023 to 2026](https://www.ons.gov.uk/businessindustryandtrade/business/businessservices/articles/artificialintelligenceinukbusinesses/2023to2026) | Office for National Statistics | 20 July 2026 | Wave 159, 15 to 28 June 2026; sample 38,637, 10,311 responses, 26.7% response rate (counts from the ONS bulletin of 2 July 2026) | BICS, businesses with 10 or more employees for most results |
| [AI adoption survey report 2026](https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/ai-adoption-survey-2026) | Department for Business, Innovation, Science and Trade (the publisher named on the gov.uk page and the report cover) | 29 October 2026 | 7 January to 21 April 2026; 3,503 interviews | Telephone survey, weighted by size and sector |
| [UK Business Data Survey 2026](https://www.gov.uk/government/statistics/uk-business-data-survey-2026/uk-business-data-survey-2026) | Department for Science, Innovation and Technology, with DCMS; fieldwork by Ipsos | 18 June 2026 | October 2025 to January 2026; 4,450 businesses | Telephone and online, weighted by size and sector |

Limits that apply to every figure on this page:

- These are sample estimates. A percentage of businesses is not a count of businesses. For the official count of UK businesses by size, see my [UK business statistics](/blog/uk-business-statistics/).
- ONS BICS excludes some industries, including agriculture, energy, public administration, public education and health, and finance and insurance. The government survey excludes public sector, education and health businesses and has no businesses with fewer than 5 employees.
- I have not joined figures from different surveys into a trend. Where a series moves, it is inside one survey.
- The latest ONS BICS bulletins after 8 October 2026 (22 October, 5 November and 19 November) carry no AI adoption figure (the 5 November bulletin mentions AI only as a supply chain concern), so 8 October 2026 is the newest I found. The Figure 1 data has one point every three months.
- The ONS bulletin attaches 51% to two size bands (250 or more in the main points and chart title, 100 or more in the body text). The section above explains why I use 250 or more.

## How to cite this page

Cite the primary source for any figure you publish. Safe wording follows this pattern:

> The ONS reported that 30% of UK businesses used at least one AI technology in September 2026 (Business Insights and Conditions Survey, Wave 165, published 8 October 2026).

To cite this reconciliation page: Sunny Patel, "UK business AI adoption statistics: ONS and government surveys", sunnypatel.co.uk/blog/uk-ai-adoption-statistics/, last checked 23 November 2026.
